Notebook[{
Cell["ExpressionToDiagram: a programmer's manual", "Title"],
Cell["The Wolfram functions of CCN String Diagrams that turn expressions into boxes, lines and arrows", "Subtitle"],
Cell["By Dara O Shayda and Noor Hoshino (\:661f\:91ce\:30cc\:30fc\:30eb) \:00b7 \:00a9 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"],
Cell["After Turaev & Virelizier, Monoidal Categories and Topological Field Theory, Chapter 2. The package is expression-to-diagram.wl, in the same folder as this notebook (Desktop/Research/Monoidal Graphical Calculus). It is the companion of diagram-to-expression.wl, which goes the other way and has its own manual (diagram-to-expression-manual.nb). The two packages share their data structures, so their functions can be chained in both directions.", "Text"],
Cell["1. Loading the package", "Section"],
Cell["Evaluate the cell below once per session. The package loads diagram-to-expression.wl from the same folder if it is not loaded yet, and puts both contexts, ExpressionToDiagram` and DiagramToExpression`, on $ContextPath. Private helpers live in ExpressionToDiagram`Private`.", "Text"],
Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{$HomeDirectory, \"Desktop\", \"Research\", \"Monoidal Graphical Calculus\", \"expression-to-diagram.wl\"}]]", "Input"],
Cell["?expressionToLD", "Input"],
Cell["Names[\"ExpressionToDiagram`*\"]", "Input"],
Cell["2. Overview: the pipeline", "Section"],
Cell["editor text   --parseExpression-->  expression\nsignature text --parseSignature-->  <|name -> {dom, cod}|>\nexpression    --typeOf----------->  {dom, cod}\nexpression    --expressionToLD--->  LD[src, layers]          (the inverse of ldToExpression)\nLD            --fromLayered------>  StringDiagram             (DiagramToExpression)\nStringDiagram --render / renderP->  Graphics                  (renderP: with arrows, caps and cups)\nexpression    --expressionToStrips-> Penrose strips           (the inverse of penroseExpr)\nidL, boxL, stackL, besideL          build an LD directly, like id, a box, \:2218 and \:2297\nshow, showP, showEq, showEqP, showTrace, showTraceP, showPenrose   picture, formula and type together", "Program"],
Cell["Shortcuts: textToLD[text, sigText] does the first four steps, expressionToDiagram[expr, sig] stops at the StringDiagram, and textToGraphics[text, sigText] draws a typed expression.", "Text"],
Cell["An expression is built from SmallCircle (\:2218), CircleTimes (\:2297), Subscript[\"id\", c] (identities) and unit (\|01d7d9). Its symbols are strings (\"f\") or the structured symbols of the built-in morphisms, for example Subscript[\"ev\", \"X\"]. The manual of DiagramToExpression, section 3.3, lists them.", "Text"],
Cell["3. Conventions", "Section"],
Cell["3.1 The editor's syntax", "Subsection"],
Cell["parseExpression reads the same text as the editor's Expression box:", "Text"],
Cell["Names are runs of letters, digits, Greek letters and the marks \:2032 ' ? \:2228 * ~ \:207b \:00b9 \:02e1 \:02b3 \:2020. A name may carry a subscript after _: a single word (ev_X) or a braced group (ev_{X\:2297Y}, \:03c4_{X,Y}, \:03c6_{i,j}). As in the editor, a braced subscript longer than one character keeps its braces in the name. editorSymbol then turns built-in names into structured symbols.", "Item"],
Cell["id_X is the identity of X; id_{X\:2297Y} is the identity of X \:2297 Y. \|01d7d9, I and 1 are the unit; id_\|01d7d9 is its identity.", "Item"],
Cell["\:1ebdv and c\:0303oev may be typed as ev~ and coev~. (x) is \:2297, -> is \:2192, and \:00b7 is \:2218.", "Item"],
Cell["Precedence, tightest first:", "Text"],
Cell["juxtaposition   g f      = g \:2218 f      (f is applied first)\n\:2297              a \:2297 b\n\:2218  and  ;       g \:2218 f    ;   f ; g  = g \:2218 f\n( \:2026 )           grouping", "Program"],
Cell["So \"g f \:2297 h\" means (g \:2218 f) \:2297 h, and \"(ev_Y \:2297 id_X*)(id_Y* \:2297 coev_X)\" is a composite of two tensor products. Nested products and composites are flattened, since \:2297 and \:2218 are associative.", "Text"],
Cell["3.2 Duals", "Subsection"],
Cell["The option \"Duals\" chooses how the types of ev, coev, \:1ebdv, c\:0303oev and of dual boxes are written:", "Text"],
Cell["\"star\" (the default, \:00a72.1.3, pivotal): ev_X : X* \:2297 X \:2192 \|01d7d9, coev_X : \|01d7d9 \:2192 X \:2297 X*, \:1ebdv_X : X \:2297 X* \:2192 \|01d7d9, c\:0303oev_X : \|01d7d9 \:2192 X* \:2297 X. The dual of a box f : X \:2192 Y is f* : Y* \:2192 X*. X** is X.", "Item"],
Cell["\"vee\" (\:00a72.1.2, left duals): ev_X : \:2228X \:2297 X \:2192 \|01d7d9, coev_X : \|01d7d9 \:2192 X \:2297 \:2228X. The dual of f is \:2228f : \:2228Y \:2192 \:2228X.", "Item"],
Cell["For a tensor colour, the dual reverses the order: (X \:2297 Y)* = Y* \:2297 X*, so ev_{X\:2297Y} : Y* \:2297 X* \:2297 X \:2297 Y \:2192 \|01d7d9.", "Item"],
Cell["3.3 Built-in types", "Subsection"],
Cell["ev_X, coev_X, ev~_X, coev~_X   as in 3.2\n\:03c4_{X,Y}      : X \:2297 Y \:2192 Y \:2297 X        (the braiding, \:00a73.1)\n\:03c4\:207b\:00b9_{Y,X}    : X \:2297 Y \:2192 Y \:2297 X        (the inverse of \:03c4_{Y,X})\n\:03c8_X, \:03c8\:207b\:00b9_X  : X \:2192 X                 (strict pivotal: X** = X)\n\:03b8\:02e1_X, \:03b8\:02b3_X, \:03b8\:02e1\:207b\:00b9_X, \:03b8\:02b3\:207b\:00b9_X : X \:2192 X   (twists, \:00a73.3)\n\:03c6_{i,j} : i* \:2192 j,  \:03c6\:207b\:00b9_{i,j} : j \:2192 i*  (Lemma 4.9(a))\nf*  (or \:2228f)  : the dual of a box f in the signature", "Program"],
Cell["3.4 Arrows", "Subsection"],
Cell["The orientation of an arc is carried by its colour: X points down (against the reading direction) and X* points up (\:00a72.1.3). renderP draws an arrowhead halfway along every line, using this rule. It draws ev and \:1ebdv as caps and coev and c\:0303oev as cups, each with an arrowhead that shows which way the arc runs: a cap whose left leg is coloured X* runs to the right. render draws no arrows: it is the unoriented picture of \:00a72.1.1\:20132.1.2.", "Text"],
Cell["4. Signatures", "Section"],
Cell["parseSignature", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["parseSignature[text]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["text: the editor's Signature box, one box per line, name : source \:2192 target, with \:2297 between colours and \|01d7d9 for the unit. Text after # or // is a comment.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["An Association name -> {source colours, target colours}.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. Split the text into lines and remove comments.", "Item"],
Cell["2. Skip empty lines, lines without \:201c:\:201d and lines that start with partition.", "Item"],
Cell["3. Split the rest at the first \:201c:\:201d into a name (spaces removed) and a type, and the type at \:2192 (or ->).", "Item"],
Cell["4. Each side is split at \:2297; parentheses and unit words are dropped.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The editor's other signature lines (pairing \:03c9 \:03a9, inverse u u\:2032, retract p j, simple i, scalar a) have no \:201c:\:201d and are ignored: they matter to the rewrite rules, not to drawing.", "Item"],
Cell["A partition line declares two boxes in the editor (p_\:03b1 and q_\:03b1). Here, write their types out as ordinary lines if you need them.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["sig1 = parseSignature[\"f : X \:2192 Y\\ng : Y \:2297 Z \:2192 \|01d7d9\\n# a comment\\npairing \:03c9 \:03a9\"]", "Input"],
Cell["5. Parsing", "Section"],
Cell["parseExpression", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["parseExpression[text]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["text: an expression in the editor's syntax (section 3.1).", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["An expression, or $Failed with the message parseExpression::syntax, which says what was expected and at which character.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. Tokenize: whitespace is skipped; \:2297 \:2218 \:00b7 ; ( ) _ { } , = \:2192 : are symbols; runs of name characters are words. Any other character is an error.", "Item"],
Cell["2. Parse by recursive descent: expr := tens ((\:2218 | ;) tens)*, tens := juxt (\:2297 juxt)*, juxt := atom atom*, atom := ( expr ) | \|01d7d9 | id_subscript | name [_subscript].", "Item"],
Cell["3. A name with its subscript is passed to editorSymbol, so built-in morphisms become structured symbols.", "Item"],
Cell["4. Nested \:2297 and \:2218 are flattened.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The parser does not need the signature: types are only checked by typeOf and expressionToLD.", "Item"],
Cell["f : X \:2192 Y inside an expression (the editor's inline typing) is not supported; declare types in the signature.", "Item"],
Cell["Messages", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["parseExpression::syntax: \:201cExpected a name at position \:2026\:201d, \:201cUnexpected symbol \:2026 at position \:2026\:201d, \:201cEmpty expression\:201d, and so on.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["parseExpression[\"(ev_Y \:2297 id_X*)(id_Y* \:2297 f \:2297 id_X*)(id_Y* \:2297 coev_X)\"]", "Input"],
Cell["parseExpression[\"g f ; h\"]", "Input"],
Cell["parseExpression[\"\:03c4_{X,Y}(f \:2297 g)\"]", "Input"],
Cell["parseExpression[\"f \:2297 \:2297 g\"]", "Input"],
Cell["parseEquation", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["parseEquation[text]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["text: an equation, with = between its sides.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The list of the parsed sides.", "Text"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["parseEquation[\"(ev_X \:2297 id_X)(id_X* \:2297 \:03c4_{X,X})(coev~_X \:2297 id_X) = \:03b8\:02e1_X\"]", "Input"],
Cell["6. Types", "Section"],
Cell["builtinType", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["builtinType[symbol, duals]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["symbol: a structured symbol or a string.", "Item"],
Cell["duals: \"star\" or \"vee\" (default \"star\").", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["{source colours, target colours} for a built-in morphism (section 3.3), or Missing[] for any other symbol.", "Text"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["builtinType[Subscript[\"ev\", \"X\:2297Y\"], \"star\"]", "Input"],
Cell["builtinType[Subscript[\"coev\", \"X\"], \"vee\"]", "Input"],
Cell["builtinType[Subscript[Superscript[\"\:03c4\", \"-1\"], \"Y,X\"]]", "Input"],
Cell["builtinType[\"f\"]", "Input"],
Cell["typeOf", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["typeOf[expr, sig, \"Duals\" -> duals]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expr: an expression.", "Item"],
Cell["sig: a signature (parseSignature).", "Item"],
Cell["\"Duals\": \"star\" (default) or \"vee\".", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["{source colours, target colours}, or $Failed with a message of expressionToLD (the type is computed by building the layered form).", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. A box's type comes, in this order, from the signature, from builtinType, or (for f* or \:2228f) from the type of f, dualized: f* : (target of f)* \:2192 (source of f)*.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["typeOf[parseExpression[\"g (f \:2297 id_Z)\"], sig1]", "Input"],
Cell["typeOf[parseExpression[\"f*\"], sig1]", "Input"],
Cell["typeOf[parseExpression[\"\:2228f\"], sig1, \"Duals\" -> \"vee\"]", "Input"],
Cell["7. Building layered forms directly", "Section"],
Cell["idL, boxL, stackL, besideL", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["idL[cs]   boxL[s, ins, outs]   stackL[l1, l2, ...]   besideL[l1, l2, ...]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["cs, ins, outs: lists of colours.", "Item"],
Cell["s: a symbol.", "Item"],
Cell["l1, l2, ...: layered forms.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["idL[cs] = LD[cs, {}]. boxL[s, ins, outs] = LD[ins, {{0, B[s, ins, outs]}}]. stackL[l1, l2] = l2 \:2218 l1, l2 on top. besideL[l1, l2] = l1 \:2297 l2, l2 on the right.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. stackL concatenates the layers. It is defined only when the target of l1 equals the source of l2; otherwise it stays unevaluated.", "Item"],
Cell["2. besideL first applies all the layers of l1, then those of l2, shifted right by the width of l1's target. So the left factor is drawn first: the same convention as the editor's Core.toLayered.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["These are the combinators the notebooks use to write diagrams by hand (sections 14\:201319 of graphical-calculus-2.1-simplify.nb).", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["stackL[boxL[\"f\", {\"X\"}, {\"Y\"}], boxL[\"g\", {\"Y\"}, {\"Z\"}]]", "Input"],
Cell["besideL[boxL[\"f\", {\"X\"}, {\"Y\"}], idL[{\"U\"}], boxL[\"h\", {\"V\"}, {}]]", "Input"],
Cell["stackL[boxL[\"f\", {\"X\"}, {\"Y\"}], boxL[\"g\", {\"W\"}, {\"Z\"}]]", "Input"],
Cell["8. From an expression to a diagram", "Section"],
Cell["expressionToLD", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expressionToLD[expr, sig, \"Duals\" -> duals]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expr: an expression.", "Item"],
Cell["sig: a signature.", "Item"],
Cell["\"Duals\": \"star\" (default) or \"vee\".", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The layered form LD[src, layers], or $Failed with a message.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. unit \:21a6 idL[{}]; Subscript[\"id\", c] \:21a6 idL of the colours of c (a tensor colour \"X\:2297Y\" gives two strands).", "Item"],
Cell["2. A box s \:21a6 boxL[s, dom, cod], with its type from section 6. A box without a type is an error (expressionToLD::untyped).", "Item"],
Cell["3. a \:2297 b \:2297 ... \:21a6 besideL of the translations of the factors.", "Item"],
Cell["4. g \:2218 f \:2218 ... \:21a6 the translations stacked from the right: f first. Each factor must start from the target of the one below; if not, expressionToLD::type names the two factors and both rows of colours.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expressionToLD is the Wolfram twin of the editor's Core.toLayered. On the 148 sides of the editor's examples without crossings, it gives exactly the same layered form, position for position and name for name, in both duals modes (the test in section 13 checks this from tests/editor-text-layered.json).", "Item"],
Cell["It inverts ldToExpression: expressionToLD[ldToExpression[ld], sig] === ld for all 148.", "Item"],
Cell["The result is not normalized. Use the engine's normalize, or simplifyTrace, to bring it to the normal form used by the rewrite rules.", "Item"],
Cell["Messages", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expressionToLD::type: \:201cThe composite g \:2218 f does not fit: the target of the right factor is \:2026, but the left factor starts from \:2026.\:201d", "Item"],
Cell["expressionToLD::untyped: \:201cThe box k has no type: add a line \"k : \:2026 \:2192 \:2026\" to the signature.\:201d", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["ld1 = expressionToLD[parseExpression[\"(ev_Y \:2297 id_X*)(id_Y* \:2297 f \:2297 id_X*)(id_Y* \:2297 coev_X)\"], sig1]", "Input"],
Cell["expressionToLD[parseExpression[\"g f\"], parseSignature[\"f : X \:2192 Y\\ng : Z \:2192 W\"]]", "Input"],
Cell["expressionToLD[parseExpression[\"k\"], sig1]", "Input"],
Cell["expressionToDiagram, textToLD, textToGraphics", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expressionToDiagram[expr, sig]   textToLD[text, sigText]   textToGraphics[text, sigText, \"Oriented\" -> True]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expr, sig: as above.", "Item"],
Cell["text, sigText: the editor's Expression and Signature boxes, as strings.", "Item"],
Cell["\"Oriented\": True for renderP (arrows), False for render. \"Duals\" as above.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A StringDiagram (fromLayered of the layered form), a layered form, or Graphics; $Failed if parsing or typing fails.", "Text"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expressionToDiagram[parseExpression[\"g (f \:2297 id_Z)\"], sig1]", "Input"],
Cell["textToGraphics[\"(ev_Y \:2297 id_X*)(id_Y* \:2297 f \:2297 id_X*)(id_Y* \:2297 coev_X)\", \"f : X \:2192 Y\"]", "Input"],
Cell["textToGraphics[\"(ev_Y \:2297 id_\:2228X)(id_\:2228Y \:2297 f \:2297 id_\:2228X)(id_\:2228Y \:2297 coev_X)\", \"f : X \:2192 Y\", \"Duals\" -> \"vee\", \"Oriented\" -> False]", "Input"],
Cell["9. Penrose strips from an expression", "Section"],
Cell["expressionToStrips", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expressionToStrips[expr, sig]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expr: an expression in Penrose form, s_n \:2218 \:2026 \:2218 s_1, where each slice s_k is a \:2297 of boxes and identities (or a single box or identity).", "Item"],
Cell["sig: a signature.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The strips {strip_1, \:2026, strip_n}, bottom to top. Each box becomes B[s, dom, cod] and each identity id_c becomes seg pieces, one per colour of c. Returns $Failed with a message if a slice is not a \:2297 of boxes and identities.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. List the slices bottom first (the factors of the composite, reversed).", "Item"],
Cell["2. In each slice, map every factor to its pieces and join them.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["It inverts penroseExpr: penroseExpr[expressionToStrips[e, sig]] gives e back when every identity is on a single colour.", "Item"],
Cell["Types use the \"star\" convention.", "Item"],
Cell["Messages", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expressionToStrips::shape: \:201cThe slice \:2026 is not a \:2297 of boxes and identities \:2026\:201d", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["strips1 = expressionToStrips[parseExpression[\"h (id_A* \:2297 g)\"], parseSignature[\"g : A \:2192 B\\nh : A* \:2297 B \:2192 C\"]]", "Input"],
Cell["penroseLD[{\"A*\", \"A\"}, strips1]", "Input"],
Cell["showPenrose[{\"A*\", \"A\"}, strips1]", "Input"],
Cell["10. Drawing", "Section"],
Cell["render", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["render[d]   render[ld]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["d: a StringDiagram; ld: a layered form (it is laid out with fromLayered first).", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Graphics: every strand is a vertical line of thickness 2.4, labelled with its colour near its loose end (or halfway); every box is a white rectangle with a black edge, labelled with its symbol (dispSym).", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. Lines: from (x, endY[lower]) to (x, endY[upper]). The label goes 0.08 to the right, 0.2 inside a loose end.", "Item"],
Cell["2. Boxes: the rectangle {x1, y \:2212 hgt/2} \:2013 {x2, y + hgt/2}, with the symbol at the centre in Times, size 13 for structured symbols and 15 otherwise.", "Item"],
Cell["3. Plot range: the bounding box of all ends and corners, at least \:22121 \:2026 1 vertically, padded by 0.45 horizontally and 0.1 vertically. The image is 62 points per unit of width.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["render shows no orientation. Use renderP for pivotal diagrams.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["render[ld1]", "Input"],
Cell["renderP", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["renderP[d]   renderP[ld]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["As for render.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Graphics of the oriented diagram (\:00a72.1.3): lines of thickness 2.2 with an arrowhead halfway, labelled with the colour without its star (an up arrow already says X*); caps and cups for ev, \:1ebdv, coev and c\:0303oev; rectangles for every other box.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. For every strand: a line and an arrowhead at its midpoint, pointing up if the colour is starred (upQ) and down otherwise. The label is lineLabel[colour]: X* is labelled X.", "Item"],
Cell["2. For a box ev or \:1ebdv with two input legs: a B\:00e9zier cap from the left leg to the right leg, rising 0.67, with an arrowhead at the top pointing right if the left leg is starred, left otherwise.", "Item"],
Cell["3. For a box coev or c\:0303oev with two output legs: a B\:00e9zier cup, falling 0.67, with an arrowhead at the bottom pointing left if the left leg is starred, right otherwise.", "Item"],
Cell["4. Every other box: a rectangle, as in render.", "Item"],
Cell["5. Plot range as in render, padded by 0.5 on the left and 0.55 on the right.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The arrowheads are drawn by head[{x, y}, dir], a small triangle of size 0.075 pointing up, down, left or right.", "Item"],
Cell["The editor's canvas follows the same rules (Figure 2.2 of the book).", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["renderP[ld1]", "Input"],
Cell["renderP[textToLD[\"(ev_X \:2297 id_X)(id_X* \:2297 \:03c4_{X,X})(coev~_X \:2297 id_X)\", \"\"]]", "Input"],
Cell["dispSym, pretty", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["dispSym[symbol]   pretty[expr]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A symbol; an expression.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The typeset form: names in italics; ev, coev, id upright with the subscript typeset; X* as X with a superscript star; \:2228X with a small upright \:2228; names starting with \:03a9 or ( upright; unit as \|01d7d9.", "Text"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["pretty[parseExpression[\"(ev_Y \:2297 id_X*)(id_Y* \:2297 f* \:2297 id_X*)\"]]", "Input"],
Cell["source, target", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["source[d]   target[d]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["d: a StringDiagram.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The \:2297, left to right, of the colours of the strands with a loose lower end (source) or upper end (target); unit if there are none.", "Text"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["{source[fromLayered[ld1]], target[fromLayered[ld1]]}", "Input"],
Cell["11. Pictures with their formulas", "Section"],
Cell["show, showP", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["show[d]   showP[d]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A StringDiagram or a layered form.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A row of three: the picture (render or renderP), the expression read back by toExpression (typeset by pretty), and the type source \:2192 target in grey.", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The formula is read back from the picture, so it shows what the picture means. Comparing it with the expression you started from checks the drawing.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["show[ld1]", "Input"],
Cell["showP[ld1]", "Input"],
Cell["showEq, showEqP", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["showEq[{d1, d2, ...}]   showEqP[{ld1, ld2, ...}]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A list of diagrams or layered forms.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The pictures in a row with = between them, and the expressions below.", "Text"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Figure 2.4: a box slides through \:1ebdv.", "Text"],
Cell["showEqP[textToLD[#, \"f : X \:2192 Y\"] & /@ StringSplit[\"ev~_Y(f \:2297 id_Y*) = ev~_X(id_X \:2297 f*)\", \"=\"]]", "Input"],
Cell["showTrace, showTraceP", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["showTrace[steps]   showTraceP[steps]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["steps: a list {{rule, ld}, \:2026}, as the engine's simplifyTrace returns it. The first rule is usually \"start\".", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The pictures of the steps with the rule names over the arrows between them, and the expressions below.", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["showTraceP draws the steps with renderP.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["showTraceP[{{\"start\", textToLD[\"(id_X \:2297 ev_X)(coev_X \:2297 id_X)\", \"\"]}, {\"(2.1) left zigzag\", idL[{\"X\"}]}}]", "Input"],
Cell["showPenrose", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["showPenrose[src, strips]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["As for penroseLD.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["renderP of the Penrose diagram beside its source, its target and the formula penroseExpr; $Failed if the strips do not fit.", "Text"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["showPenrose[{\"A*\", \"A\"}, strips1]", "Input"],
Cell["12. How the translators relate to the editor's JavaScript", "Section"],
Cell["Core.parseSig \:2194 parseSignature (the editor also reads pairing, inverse, retract, simple, partition and scalar lines).", "Item"],
Cell["Core.parse \:2194 parseExpression (the editor also accepts inline types f : X \:2192 Y and the dagger \:2020).", "Item"],
Cell["Core.toLayered \:2194 expressionToLD: the same layered form on all 148 example sides (section 13).", "Item"],
Cell["Core.toDiagram (layout on the canvas) \:2194 fromLayered; the canvas renderer \:2194 render and renderP.", "Item"],
Cell["13. Tests", "Section"],
Cell["Each line should be True. The last two lines compare with the editor itself: tests/editor-text-layered.json holds, for every side of the editor's examples without crossings, the text, the signature, the duals mode and the layered form computed by the editor's JavaScript (Core.toLayered).", "Text"],
Cell["etl = Import[FileNameJoin[{$HomeDirectory, \"Desktop\", \"Research\", \"Monoidal Graphical Calculus\", \"tests\", \"editor-text-layered.json\"}], \"RawJSON\"];\n<|\n \"parse: juxtaposition, \:2297 and ;\" -> (parseExpression[\"g f ; h\"] === SmallCircle[\"h\", \"g\", \"f\"] && parseExpression[\"a \:2297 b c\"] === CircleTimes[\"a\", SmallCircle[\"b\", \"c\"]]),\n \"parse: subscripts and built-ins\" -> (parseExpression[\"\:03c4_{X,Y} ev~_{X\:2297Y} \:03b8\:02e1_X\"] === SmallCircle[Subscript[\"\:03c4\", \"X,Y\"], Subscript[OverTilde[\"ev\"], \"X\:2297Y\"], Subscript[\"\:03b8\:02e1\", \"X\"]]),\n \"parse: errors give $Failed\" -> (Quiet[parseExpression[\"f \:2297 \:2297 g\"]] === $Failed && Quiet[parseExpression[\"\"]] === $Failed),\n \"signature\" -> (parseSignature[\"f : X \:2192 Y\\ng : Y \:2297 Z \:2192 \|01d7d9\\npairing a b\"] === <|\"f\" -> {{\"X\"}, {\"Y\"}}, \"g\" -> {{\"Y\", \"Z\"}, {}}|>),\n \"types of duals\" -> (typeOf[parseExpression[\"f*\"], sig1] === {{\"Y*\"}, {\"X*\"}} && typeOf[parseExpression[\"\:2228f\"], sig1, \"Duals\" -> \"vee\"] === {{\"\:2228Y\"}, {\"\:2228X\"}}),\n \"type errors give $Failed\" -> (Quiet[expressionToLD[parseExpression[\"f f\"], sig1]] === $Failed && Quiet[expressionToLD[parseExpression[\"k\"], sig1]] === $Failed),\n \"Figure 2.3 as a layered form\" -> (ld1 === LD[{\"Y*\"}, {{1, B[Subscript[\"coev\", \"X\"], {}, {\"X\", \"X*\"}]}, {1, B[\"f\", {\"X\"}, {\"Y\"}]}, {0, B[Subscript[\"ev\", \"Y\"], {\"Y*\", \"Y\"}, {}]}}]),\n \"round trip: expression \:2192 LD \:2192 expression\" -> (ldToExpression[ld1] === parseExpression[\"(ev_Y \:2297 id_X*)(id_Y* \:2297 f \:2297 id_X*)(id_Y* \:2297 coev_X)\"]),\n \"Penrose round trip\" -> (penroseExpr[strips1] === parseExpression[\"h (id_A* \:2297 g)\"]),\n \"drawing gives Graphics\" -> (Head[renderP[ld1]] === Graphics && Head[render[ld1]] === Graphics),\n \"the editor's 148 layered forms, exactly\" -> AllTrue[etl, With[{l = Quiet[textToLD[#text, #sig, \"Duals\" -> #duals]]}, l =!= $Failed && ldToJSON[l][\"src\"] === #src && ldToJSON[l][\"ls\"] === #ls] &],\n \"expressionToLD inverts ldToExpression on the same 148\" -> AllTrue[etl, With[{ld = jsonToLD[<|\"src\" -> #src, \"ls\" -> (<|\"a\" -> #[[1]], \"name\" -> #[[2]], \"ins\" -> #[[3]], \"outs\" -> #[[4]]|> & /@ #ls)|>]},\n     Quiet[expressionToLD[ldToExpression[ld], parseSignature[#sig], \"Duals\" -> #duals]] === ld] &]\n|>", "Input"],
Cell["14. Index of public symbols", "Section"],
